---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Setup = `import turtle

turtle.forward(100)
turtle.right(90)
turtle.forward(100)

turtle.done()`;

const Square = `import turtle

for side in range(4):
    turtle.forward(100)
    turtle.right(90)

turtle.done()`;

const Colors = `import turtle

turtle.speed(0)
turtle.pencolor("cyan")
turtle.width(3)

for side in range(6):
    turtle.forward(120)
    turtle.right(60)

turtle.done()`;

const Spiral = `import turtle

turtle.speed(0)

for step in range(90):
    turtle.forward(step * 3)
    turtle.right(89)

turtle.done()`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/foce/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Library Exploration: Turtle Graphics</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">Optional &mdash; Extra Credit</h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Is</h2>

          <p>
            Everything you have written so far prints text. <b>Turtle</b> draws
            pictures instead. It comes with Python &mdash; nothing to install.
          </p>

          <p>
            You control a pen that moves around a window. Tell it to go forward,
            turn, and change color, and it leaves a line behind it. That is the
            whole idea.
          </p>

          <p>
            This pairs with <b>Activity 1.16 For Loops</b> on purpose. Drawing a
            square by hand takes eight lines. Drawing it with a loop takes three,
            and once you can loop, you can draw things that would be miserable to
            write out by hand.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="setup">
          <h2>Getting Started</h2>

          <p>
            Create a file named <b class="text-sn-theme">le-turtle.py</b> and run
            this:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Setup}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="le-turtle.py"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            A window should open and draw two lines meeting at a right angle.{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-cyan">turtle.done()</b> on the last line is what
            keeps the window from closing the instant the program ends &mdash;
            leave it off and you will see a flash of nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>If no window appears at all</b>, turtle needs a piece of Python
            called <b>tkinter</b>, and some installations leave it out. Tell me and
            we will sort it out &mdash; it is not something you did wrong.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="explore">
          <h2>Work Through These</h2>

          <h4>1. A square, with a loop</h4>

          <p>
            Four sides, four identical turns. That is a <code>for</code> loop
            wanting to happen.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Square}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="le-turtle.py"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Note that <code>side</code> is never used inside the loop. That is
            fine &mdash; sometimes you only want the loop to repeat, not to count.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h4>2. Color, width, and speed</h4>

          <Code
            code={Colors}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="le-turtle.py"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            <code>turtle.speed(0)</code> means "as fast as possible" &mdash; you
            will want it once your drawings get bigger. Try changing the 6 and the
            60 together and see what shapes you get.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Here is the pattern worth knowing:</b> for any regular shape, the
            turn is <b class="text-sn-cyan">360 divided by the number of sides</b>.
            Six sides, 60 degrees. Eight sides, 45. Work out why.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h4>3. A spiral</h4>

          <p>
            Now use the loop variable instead of ignoring it. Each pass draws a
            slightly longer line than the last.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Spiral}
            frame="code"
            lang="python"
            title="le-turtle.py"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Change the <b>89</b> to <b>90</b> and run it again. One degree of
            difference changes the whole picture &mdash; that is worth staring at
            for a moment.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="make">
          <h2>Then Make Something</h2>

          <p>
            Draw something of your own. Ideas, in rough order of difficulty:
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>A triangle, a hexagon, and an octagon side by side</li>
            <li>A five-pointed star &mdash; the turn is not 72 degrees, work it out</li>
            <li>A row of squares, using a loop inside a loop</li>
            <li>Your initials</li>
            <li>
              Ask the user how many sides they want, then draw that shape
              &mdash; now you are combining turtle with <code>input()</code> and
              casting
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b>To submit:</b> your <code>.py</code> file and a screenshot of what
            it drew.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Go Draw Something
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
